# Line endings, decided here rather than by whoever happens to clone.
#
# Without this file the answer depends on each contributor's `core.autocrlf`, and the failure it
# produces is unhelpful in a specific way: a shell script committed with CRLF from a Windows
# machine fails on Linux and macOS with `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M`, which names a
# file that plainly exists and an interpreter that plainly works. Nobody's first guess is line
# endings.
#
# Everything text is normalised to LF in the repository. What a working copy looks like is then
# git's business, but what is COMMITTED is the same for everyone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Executable by construction. A CRLF here is not a cosmetic diff, it is a script that will not
# run on two of the three platforms this is meant to work on.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf

# Binary, so git neither converts nor tries to diff them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
*.gguf binary
*.whl binary

# WHAT IS GENERATED SAYS SO. GitHub's language bar reads bytes, and this repository ships 3.4MB
# of HTML against 2.3MB of Python, so it called itself a 60% HTML project. None of that HTML is
# written: `out/*.html` is written by the reporter, and every `site/<lang>/index.html` is
# written by tools/i18n.py from site/index.html, which is the one page a person edits and the
# one left counted.
#
# `linguist-generated` changes two things and nothing else. It takes these files out of the
# language statistics, and it folds them in a diff, which is the more useful half: a
# regenerated 74KB page in every pull request buries the source change that caused it.
#
# It does NOT hide them. `out/` is the evidence this project stakes its claims on, the page
# links straight into it, and every file stays browsable at the same URL. Saying a derived
# file is derived is not the same as hiding it.
out/*.html linguist-generated=true
site/*/index.html linguist-generated=true
